-- title:
--   Invertibility of ideal sheaves is preserved by open immersions
-- statement:
--   Let $X$ and $Y$ be schemes and let $I$ be an ideal sheaf datum on $X$ (a `Scheme.IdealSheafData`, i.e. a compatible family of ideals $I.\mathrm{ideal}\,U \subseteq \Gamma(X,U)$ indexed by the affine opens $U$ of $X$). Assume $I$ satisfies the predicate `IsInvertible`: for every point $x \in X$ there are an affine open $U \subseteq X$ and a section $f \in \Gamma(X,U)$ with $x$ in the basic open $X.\mathrm{basicOpen}\,f$, together with an element $g$ of $\Gamma(X, X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\,f)$ which is a non-zero-divisor and satisfies $I.\mathrm{ideal}\,(X.\mathrm{affineBasicOpen}\,f) = (g)$, the ideal generated by $g$. Let $j \colon Y \to X$ be a morphism of schemes which is an open immersion. Then the pulled-back ideal sheaf datum $I.\mathrm{comap}\,j$ on $Y$ is again `IsInvertible`: every point of $Y$ has an affine open neighbourhood and a section on it whose associated affine basic open carries a single non-zero-divisor generator of the ideal of $I.\mathrm{comap}\,j$ there.
--
--   This is the statement that the local principality condition defining invertible ideal sheaves (equivalently, effective Cartier divisors given by a local non-zero-divisor generator) is stable under restriction to an open subscheme. It is used throughout the relative Cartier divisor and relative Picard group material, for instance when producing affine charts on which a given invertible ideal sheaf is generated by one element.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_Scheme_IdealSheafData_IsInvertible_comap_of_isOpenImmersion.lean

